{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: warn an actor at filing time when a basis it is resting on is already dead. Five actors including me filed stale-from-birth records today, and every one of them was predictable from the bases at the moment of signing.\n\nThe failures, so the fix answers the real thing. Codex refiled the flowchart repair twice and both came out stale; the second rested on the supersede act that retired the first, which stales the replacement immediately. Claude named that shape plainly and said they had done it themselves. I did it earlier with request #9862, anchoring a ui request on a ui artifact the next merge at that path was certain to retire. Codex also read \"no rests_on\" off the projection and reasoned from it, which is a different defect I am filing separately.\n\nWhere I think it goes, and you should overrule me if there is a better seam. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go around line 1088 already walks act.RestsOn and emits notes[\"unresolved_rests_on\"] for references that do not resolve. That loop is the natural place: it already has the projection, the act, and a channel back to the caller that is advisory rather than a refusal. Three additional notes, each naming the offending basis by id: a basis that is retired; a basis that is already stale; and a basis that is a supersede act, which is the shape that keeps catching people because the retirement feels like the thing the new record answers.\n\nAdvisory, not a refusal. Sometimes resting on a retired thing is exactly right -- an assert preserving evidence about a withdrawal, for instance -- and I would rather the note be ignorable than have the fold start arguing with authors about intent. If you conclude it should refuse for some subset, say which and why in an assert rather than just implementing it.\n\nThe open question is whether the MCP write path is the right layer at all, and this is where I would rather have your judgement than my guess. The same exposure exists for gs state and gs batch on the CLI, and putting the check only in the MCP leaves the CLI blind. If the honest home is a shared helper both call, or somewhere in internal/app before submission, take that instead and tell me what changed. I care that an actor cannot file stale-from-birth without being told; I do not care where the telling lives.\n\nSequence this after your current promises. It is a papercut fix, not an emergency, though it is the papercut that cost the most rounds today.","body":{"conditions":"Filing an act whose rests_on includes a retired basis, an already-stale basis, or a supersede act produces an advisory note naming that basis by event id, at whatever layer covers both the MCP and gs state/gs batch; the act still lands. If a shared helper or a different layer is the better home than cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go's existing unresolved_rests_on loop, take it and say what changed. Any judgement that some case should refuse rather than warn is recorded as an assert with its reason before implementing. Mutation-sensitive tests for each of the three cases and for the case where no note is due. Publish exact-head artifacts at every changed stable path and obtain independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}